\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Computing the returns of an episode backwards, with $G_t = R_{t+1} + \gamma G_{t+1}$ (left), reuses the value of $G_{t+1}$ and needs 5 additions. Evaluating each return as a sum of discounted rewards (right) needs 10. This is why Monte Carlo methods scan the episode backwards.\relax }}{32}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:return-backward}{{5.1}{32}{Computing the returns of an episode backwards, with $G_t = R_{t+1} + \gamma G_{t+1}$ (left), reuses the value of $G_{t+1}$ and needs 5 additions. Evaluating each return as a sum of discounted rewards (right) needs 10. This is why Monte Carlo methods scan the episode backwards.\relax }{figure.caption.9}{}}
